{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y5pEpK3Wuf6LsA6vtzdoFB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\begin{abstract}\n2\tTraining-free LLM information extraction---prompting models with target schemas without fine-tuning---degrades sharply as schema complexity grows: NER recall drops from 0.72 to 0.42 with 64 candidate types, and RE accuracy falls from 85.7\\% to 50.4\\% with 80 relations (sentence-level) and from 88.0\\% to 38.7\\% with 96 relations (document-level). Prior work conflates type count with type confusability, leaving the mechanisms unclear. We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating these factors across three IE tasks and four datasets, introducing the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) and two experimental paradigms: count curves that vary schema size while randomizing confusability, and BCS-controlled experiments that fix size while varying confusability. Our experiments reveal two degradation channels: a count channel that suppresses recall in NER and RE, but reverses direction in MAVEN (168 event types) due to centrality confounds in same-domain schemas as candidate types increase, and a confusability channel where semantically similar types trigger intra-batch substitution errors---cleanly separable in RE (a closed-set classification task); in NER, confusability additionally impairs entity detection. The BCS--accuracy relationship is robust across schema sizes and prompt designs: the regression slope is consistent between independent 16-type and within-pool 80-type evaluations (permutation $p = 0.60$), with the quartile gap reaching $-$5.3\\,pp (95\\% CI: [$-$7.4, $-$3.2]), though pairwise effect sizes are modest ($r^2 = 1.3\\text{--}1.7\\%$) on the independent 16-relation pool. On MAVEN's same-domain schema (168 event types), per-type confusability reverses direction due to a centrality confound, though pairwise confusability remains significant. Both channels replicate on Llama-3.1-8B (count $r = 0.725$; confusability $\\Delta = -27$\\,pp), with high-BCS accuracy converging to a floor consistent across both tested models. Our confusability-aware decomposition yields an informative negative result for NER---merge amplification destroys precision across all strategies---but succeeds for RE, where confusability-aware batch composition recovers +1.4\\,pp accuracy over random batching, demonstrating a directionally consistent application of our diagnostic framework.\n3\t\\end{abstract}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013d49a2oqqfFWwjsZEVeZkX","session_id":"cb6965cd-db2a-41dc-ac23-0e72d1208059","uuid":"085d5c4e-d1e7-43ba-9fc5-1ec4261a16dd","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:18:43.262Z"}
