{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013d49a2oqqfFWwjsZEVeZkX","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the assessment:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\n**Strengths acknowledged:**\n- Well-designed controlled experiments with careful within-pool controls and appropriate statistical methods\n- Exceptional transparency about negative results, modest effect sizes, and non-significant findings\n- The centrality confound analysis (MAVEN) is a genuine methodological insight, well-connected to the embedding anisotropy literature\n- Cross-model/cross-dataset validation provides reasonable breadth\n\n**Major weaknesses identified:**\n1. The \"multi-channel degradation framework\" formalizes well-known intuitions (similar categories confuse classifiers, more categories reduce accuracy) without substantial novelty -- framing as \"discovery\" overstates the contribution\n2. Pairwise SCS explains only 1.3-1.7% of variance; typical-batch gaps are ~3pp -- the metric's practical utility is questionable\n3. All practical interventions fail (SDGD) or are non-significant (RE batching at p~0.19, ranking 13th/15 random seeds)\n4. Incomplete related work: missing fine-grained classification confusion literature, constrained decoding approaches, cognitive science foundations, and insufficient engagement with code-generation approaches (GoLLIE) that sidestep confusability by design\n5. Single primary model (Qwen3-32B) limits generalizability; Channel 2 not reproduced on NER for Llama-8B\n\n**Recommendation:** Solid work for Findings track but below the bar for EMNLP main. Would need mechanistic depth (attention-level analysis), a higher-power confusability metric, or a working practical mitigation to reach main conference acceptance.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T073533/median/review_report.md`"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a802b76b50c1643e5 (use SendMessage with to: 'a802b76b50c1643e5' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 65649\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 149490</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb6965cd-db2a-41dc-ac23-0e72d1208059","uuid":"51dc7caa-8e2a-4678-9049-789c82883d33","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:20:49.995Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work**, **framing/positioning**, and **contribution significance**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Characterizing and Diagnosing Schema-Induced Failure Modes in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper proposes a diagnostic framework for understanding why training-free LLM IE degrades on complex schemas. It identifies two \"orthogonal\" degradation channels: (1) type count→recall loss via attention dilution, (2) type confusability→substitution errors via semantic interference. It introduces Schema Confusability Score (SCS = cosine similarity of type embeddings), an error taxonomy (IntraSub/ExtraSub/Boundary/Hallucination), and Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD). \n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Multi-channel degradation framework (count→recall, confusability→type confusion)\n2. Pairwise confusability validation (SCS vs empirical confusion, ρ=0.17-0.31, R²=3-9%)\n3. Systematic negative results on schema decomposition (SDGD fails, random > SDGD, merge amplification)\n4. Prescriptive schema design guidelines (decision tree in Section 5.3)\n\n**Related work covered (Section 2, very brief ~half page):**\n- Training-free IE: GPT-NER, UniNER, GoLLIE\n- Schema complexity: \"Lost in the Middle\" (Liu et al., 2024)\n- Schema decomposition: LLM-PEE\n\n**Key results:**\n- Error taxonomy: IntraSub correlates with BCS (ρ=1.0 NER, ρ=0.954 RE) — but only 4 bins\n- SCS validation: statistically significant but R²=3-9% (weak signal)\n- SDGD fails everywhere: single-pass F1=0.445 vs SDGD-zero 0.241 on Few-NERD; single-pass 0.245 vs SDGD-zero 0.152 on MAVEN\n- Random batching beats SDGD (p=0.03)\n- SDGD-cal marginal improvement (+2.7pp Few-NERD, +0.9pp MAVEN) confirms problem is merge, not estimation\n- Schema design guidelines: 5 rules (prefer single-pass, use random batching if needed, avoid per-type, prefer hierarchical schemas, don't rely on SCS alone)\n- DocRED BCS effect inconclusive (p=0.14-0.21)\n- 2×2 interaction non-significant (p=0.50)\n\n**Experimental setup:**\n- Single model: Qwen3-32B only\n- 3 datasets: Few-NERD (NER, 66 types), DocRED (RE, 96 types), MAVEN (ED, 168 types)\n- No comparison with other schema decomposition methods beyond UniNER-style per-type\n- SCS uses only cosine similarity of embeddings (type name + definition)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: minor/major/critical)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Assessment of contribution significance**\n6. **Preliminary score** (1-6) with confidence (1-5)\n\nFocus on:\n- Is the \"multi-channel degradation framework\" genuinely novel, or does it formalize well-known intuitions?\n- Is the error taxonomy a methodological contribution or descriptive analysis?\n- Do negative results constitute sufficient contribution for a top venue?\n- Is the related work adequate? Major missing references?\n- Are the schema design guidelines actionable and well-supported?\n- Does the paper advance understanding beyond \"similar things are confused more often\"?\n- Is framing as \"diagnostic framework\" appropriate for what is essentially an empirical analysis paper?\n- How does this compare to concurrent work on structured extraction (e.g., code-generation approaches like GoLLIE that avoid schema confusion by design)?\n","agentId":"a802b76b50c1643e5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the assessment:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\n**Strengths acknowledged:**\n- Well-designed controlled experiments with careful within-pool controls and appropriate statistical methods\n- Exceptional transparency about negative results, modest effect sizes, and non-significant findings\n- The centrality confound analysis (MAVEN) is a genuine methodological insight, well-connected to the embedding anisotropy literature\n- Cross-model/cross-dataset validation provides reasonable breadth\n\n**Major weaknesses identified:**\n1. The \"multi-channel degradation framework\" formalizes well-known intuitions (similar categories confuse classifiers, more categories reduce accuracy) without substantial novelty -- framing as \"discovery\" overstates the contribution\n2. Pairwise SCS explains only 1.3-1.7% of variance; typical-batch gaps are ~3pp -- the metric's practical utility is questionable\n3. All practical interventions fail (SDGD) or are non-significant (RE batching at p~0.19, ranking 13th/15 random seeds)\n4. Incomplete related work: missing fine-grained classification confusion literature, constrained decoding approaches, cognitive science foundations, and insufficient engagement with code-generation approaches (GoLLIE) that sidestep confusability by design\n5. Single primary model (Qwen3-32B) limits generalizability; Channel 2 not reproduced on NER for Llama-8B\n\n**Recommendation:** Solid work for Findings track but below the bar for EMNLP main. Would need mechanistic depth (attention-level analysis), a higher-power confusability metric, or a working practical mitigation to reach main conference acceptance.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T073533/median/review_report.md`"}],"totalDurationMs":149490,"totalTokens":65649,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3381,"cache_read_input_tokens":61801,"output_tokens":466,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3381},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":466,"cache_read_input_tokens":61801,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3381,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3381,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a1d69f340e08c49a0","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MeJqX3oCo7TesFn7RTGZr7","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":77126,"tool_uses":27,"duration_ms":174489},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"4c3e605b-e37a-4d1c-b3e3-8738c8bdd6db","session_id":"cb6965cd-db2a-41dc-ac23-0e72d1208059"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GspQaKZnXY7AjT3E7WZQ9i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ApUNd9QFT2XT1kGR7CLGgk","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"schema-dag-extraction","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260516T073533","overall_score":"Weak Reject (3/6)","report":"<![CDATA[---\ndream_id: dream_20260516T073533\ntier: worst\noverall_score: Weak Reject (3/6)\nreviewer: R1 (Methodology & Experiments)\nconfidence: 4/5\n---\n\n# R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments Focus\n\n**Paper:** \"Does Type Confusability Drive Schema-Induced Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction?\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper conducts a controlled empirical study isolating type count from type confusability as independent drivers of performance degradation in training-free (zero-shot) LLM information extraction. Using four datasets (Few-NERD, FewRel, DocRED, MAVEN) across NER, RE, and ED tasks, the authors introduce the Schema Confusability Score (SCS/BCS) and propose a two-channel degradation model: count drives recall decline, while confusability drives type substitution errors. The paper reports an informative negative result for confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) in NER, and a small (+1.4pp, non-significant) gain for RE batch composition. While the experimental design is principled, the paper's core claims rest on a mix of modest effects, underpowered tests, and results that partially contradict the main thesis.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Principled controlled experimental design.** The two-paradigm approach (count curves + BCS-controlled experiments) is well-motivated and represents a genuine methodological improvement over prior work that conflates count and confusability. The within-pool control (drawing 16-rel subsets from the 80-rel pool) to isolate confusability from pool composition is particularly well thought out.\n\n- **Honest and transparent reporting throughout.** The authors do not hide inconvenient results: the non-significant factorial test (p=0.50), MAVEN's reversed count curve, SDGD's failure, the non-significant batch composition gain (+1.4pp, p~0.19), and the modest pairwise r^2 of 1.3-1.7% are all reported openly. The decisions log (D019, D026, D043) confirms these were not cherry-picked away. This level of transparency is commendable.\n\n- **Error taxonomy is the strongest evidence.** The IntraSub monotonic scaling with BCS (rho=1.0 NER, rho=0.954 RE) computed over n=148 and n=154 batches respectively is compelling. The observation that 100% of RE type confusion errors are intra-batch provides genuine mechanistic insight. Cross-task replication on both NER and RE strengthens this finding substantially.\n\n- **Centrality confound analysis is a useful methodological contribution.** Identifying that per-type BCS aggregation conflates confusability with centrality on same-domain schemas (MAVEN), with residualized analysis confirming the raw positive correlation vanishes under joint control (p>0.2), is a finding the community can use. The demonstration that pairwise SCS remains valid while per-type aggregation fails is informative.\n\n- **Cross-model validation on RE.** The Llama-3.1-8B replication showing BCS gaps of -27.1pp (count=8) and -23.9pp (count=16), with high-BCS accuracy converging to ~51% on both models, is good evidence that confusability effects are not model-specific for RE.\n\n- **DocRED extension strengthens generalizability.** The document-level RE replication with BCS slopes of -0.71 (steeper than FewRel) and R^2=0.53 confirms the pattern extends beyond sentence-level classification.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n### Critical\n\n- **W1: The \"orthogonal channels\" claim is unsupported by the evidence.** The paper claims count and confusability are \"orthogonal\" degradation channels, but the only direct test of this -- the 2x2 factorial (Section 4.2 / Appendix D) -- is non-significant (z=-0.67, p=0.50). A non-significant interaction test with n~13 batches per cell, BCS range compressed to 0.79-0.87, and count span of only 6 vs 12 cannot distinguish true additivity from inability to detect interaction. The permutation test on cross-pool slopes (p=0.598) tests slope *equality*, not independence of the two factors. Absent a properly powered interaction test, the \"orthogonal\" framing in the title-level claim (\"two degradation channels\") is not warranted. **Severity: Critical** -- the two-channel decomposition is the paper's central organizing claim.\n\n- **W2: Pairwise effect sizes are too small to support the headline claims.** r=0.112 (Few-NERD) and r=0.129 (MAVEN) correspond to r^2=1.3% and 1.7% -- SCS explains essentially none of the variance in type confusion at the pairwise level. The paper acknowledges this but then pivots to extreme-batch comparisons (31.2pp gap) to argue for \"practical significance.\" This conflation is problematic: the extreme-batch gap reflects hand-selected type compositions at the tails of the BCS distribution, not the typical schema designer's experience. The within-pool typical gap is only 2.2pp (95% CI: [0.4, 4.0]) -- barely above noise for practical schema optimization. The paper's framing oscillates between claiming the pairwise signal is \"modest but significant\" and leveraging the extreme-batch gap as if it represents typical operating conditions. **Severity: Critical** -- the disconnect between pairwise r^2~1.5% and the 31.2pp extreme-batch headline number makes the contribution hard to assess.\n\n### Major\n\n- **W3: Cross-model validation is incomplete for Channel 2 on NER.** The paper acknowledges that \"Channel 2 was not robustly detectable at 8B scale\" for NER (Limitations item 1). This means the core two-channel claim is validated on only one model for NER. The RE validation is strong (Llama replicates), but NER is the primary task in the paper. The floor-effect explanation is plausible but not verified -- was the count range insufficient, or does confusability genuinely not matter for smaller models on NER? This asymmetry weakens the cross-task generalization claim.\n\n- **W4: MAVEN results partially contradict the main thesis and the explanation is post-hoc.** MAVEN's count curve goes in the opposite direction (F1 increases with count), and per-type BCS correlates positively with F1. The centrality confound explanation is reasonable, but it means Channel 1 (count -> recall decline) fails on 1 of 4 datasets, and Channel 2 (confusability -> degradation) fails at the per-type level on 1 of 3 datasets. The pairwise signal survives on MAVEN (MiniLM r=0.129), but only with MiniLM -- Qwen3 pairwise is non-significant (r=-0.007, p=0.41). This means the pairwise signal is embedding-dependent on MAVEN, requiring the right embedding model to detect confusability -- a significant practical limitation that undermines the claim of a general phenomenon.\n\n- **W5: BCS-controlled design has residual confounds that are acknowledged but not resolved.** The paper states: \"high-BCS types may differ in other properties (definition granularity, frequency)\" (Section 4.3). The within-pool analysis partially mitigates this, but the 80-pool 16-sub quartile Delta CI crosses zero ([-8.17, +0.38]), meaning the confusability effect is not significant in the most tightly controlled condition. The significant regression slope (-0.401, CI excludes zero) rescues the finding, but the tension between the two analyses (quartile non-significant, slope significant) suggests the effect is fragile and sensitive to analytic choices.\n\n- **W6: Error taxonomy bins are post-hoc with only 4-5 levels.** The IntraSub rho=1.0 (NER) is computed over 4 BCS bins -- with 4 ranked values, rho=1.0 is obtained whenever the ordering is monotonic, which requires only that the 4 bin means are ordered. This is suggestive but not a rigorous statistical test. The RE rho=0.954 over 5 bins is similarly constrained. The rho values are computed over the n=148/154 individual batches (not bins), which is better, but the binning thresholds appear to be chosen post-hoc to create visually compelling monotonic trends. Were different binning schemes tested? How sensitive is the monotonicity to bin boundary choices?\n\n- **W7: The confusability-aware RE batch composition result is non-significant and relies on oracle routing.** The +1.4pp gain (p~0.19) does not reach significance. More critically, as noted in decision D043, the experiment uses oracle routing -- each sample is routed to the batch containing its gold type. This means there is no merge step, and the \"no merge amplification\" claim is vacuously true. The 29.8pp gain over single-pass is purely a count effect (80 -> ~10 types per batch), not a confusability effect. This section overstates the practical contribution.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **W8: DocRED confusability study uses only n=5 batches per condition (count curve) and n~25-27 batches for BCS-controlled.** While the BCS-controlled results are significant, the count curve with n=5 random batches per level has high sampling variance. The paper does not report CIs for the count curve, making it hard to assess whether the monotonic decline is robust or within noise.\n\n- **W9: FewRel evaluation combines train + val splits.** This is acknowledged in Limitations, but it means the 80-relation pool includes relations the model may have seen during pretraining (Wikidata relations in training data). The paper cannot rule out differential memorization as a confound -- some relations may be easier because the model has seen more examples during pretraining, not because of confusability.\n\n- **W10: Single prompt seed (42) for all experiments.** Temperature=0 with greedy decoding reduces stochasticity, but the choice of seed can still affect vLLM's batching and scheduling. No seed sensitivity analysis is provided.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Interaction test power analysis.** What sample size would be needed to detect a meaningful interaction (e.g., 5pp) at 80% power? Given the observed cell means (0.33/0.31/0.32/0.28), was the 2x2 design fundamentally underpowered, or is the interaction genuinely absent?\n\n2. **Typical-batch magnitude.** The within-pool typical BCS gap is 2.2pp (Table 5). For a practitioner deciding whether to optimize schema confusability, is 2.2pp worth the engineering cost? How does this compare to gains from better prompt engineering (v1 vs v2 yields ~5.7pp change)?\n\n3. **Error taxonomy bin sensitivity.** Were the 4 BCS bins for NER (solo/low/mid/high) determined before or after seeing the data? What happens with 3 bins, 5 bins, or continuous BCS as predictor?\n\n4. **MAVEN Qwen3 pairwise failure.** The pairwise SCS-confusion correlation is significant with MiniLM (r=0.129) but non-significant with Qwen3 (r=-0.007) on MAVEN. Why does the extraction model's own embeddings fail to capture the confusability signal on this dataset? This seems to directly undermine the theoretical motivation for SCS.\n\n5. **Oracle routing in RE batch composition.** If oracle routing is removed (i.e., samples are assigned to batches via a classifier or heuristic), what happens to the +1.4pp gain? Is the confusability-aware result purely an artifact of knowing the gold labels at test time?\n\n6. **Cluster bootstrap CIs.** The paper notes that cluster bootstrap (accounting for non-independence of pairs sharing a type) yields wider CIs. For Few-NERD, [0.069, 0.219] vs pair-level [0.076, 0.156]. Were all primary analyses also computed with cluster bootstrap? If the pairwise r=0.112 is the main evidence, the appropriate CI is the wider one.\n\n7. **Prompt v1 fabricated offsets.** The paper states v1 prompts produce fabricated character offsets in 96.5% of cases. Does this fabrication correlate with BCS? If the model spends capacity fabricating offsets, this could introduce a systematic confound in v1 experiments.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Properly powered factorial test.** The 2x2 design (Section 4.2) should be redone with adequate sample sizes (n>=50 per cell), wider count range (e.g., 8 vs 32), and wider BCS range. Without this, the \"orthogonal channels\" claim is aspirational.\n\n2. **Continuous BCS regression for error taxonomy.** Rather than binning BCS into 4-5 levels, regress IntraSub rate on continuous BCS with bootstrap CIs on the slope. This would avoid post-hoc binning concerns and provide a more rigorous test of monotonicity.\n\n3. **A second open-weight model for NER Channel 2.** The Llama-8B failure could be due to model capacity, but a mid-sized model (e.g., Llama-3.1-70B or Qwen2.5-14B) would disambiguate floor effects from genuine absence of confusability effects.\n\n4. **Ablation on SCS using type definitions vs. type names.** SCS currently uses only type name strings. Many confusable types have distinctive definitions (e.g., \"parent organization\" vs \"subsidiary\" are similar names but distinct concepts). Computing SCS on definitions could either strengthen or weaken the pairwise signal, and would test whether the measure captures the right notion of similarity.\n\n5. **Realistic (non-oracle) routing for RE batch composition.** The oracle routing assumption makes the RE decomposition result uninterpretable for practical use. A realistic router (e.g., a lightweight classifier or embedding-based assignment) would make the finding actionable.\n\n6. **Effect of in-context examples.** The paper tests only zero-shot. Even 1-2 demonstrations per type could disambiguate confusable types, potentially eliminating Channel 2. This would clarify whether confusability is inherent to the task or specific to the zero-shot setting.\n\n7. **Proprietary model validation.** All experiments use open-weight models. Given that GPT-4 and Claude are the most common deployment targets for training-free IE, at least one proprietary model comparison (even on a subset of experiments) would substantially strengthen generalizability.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Preliminary Score\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n\n### Justification\n\n**For acceptance:**\n- Principled experimental design that properly isolates variables\n- Error taxonomy (IntraSub rho>=0.95) is genuinely compelling mechanistic evidence\n- Centrality confound finding is a useful methodological contribution\n- Transparent, honest reporting of negative and weak results\n- Cross-model RE validation is strong\n\n**Against acceptance:**\n- The \"orthogonal channels\" organizing claim rests on an underpowered null result (p=0.50), which is the paper's central thesis\n- Pairwise effect sizes (r^2~1.5%) are too small for practical significance; the extreme-batch gap (31.2pp) creates a misleading impression of the typical effect magnitude (2.2pp)\n- MAVEN contradicts both channels at some analysis level, and the pairwise signal is embedding-dependent (works with MiniLM, fails with Qwen3)\n- Cross-model NER Channel 2 validation failed\n- The only actionable result (RE batch composition) is non-significant (p~0.19) and uses oracle routing, making it vacuously free of merge issues\n- The 80-pool within-pool quartile Delta CI crosses zero in the most tightly controlled analysis\n- No properly powered interaction test exists anywhere in the paper\n\n**Overall:** The paper is a careful, honest empirical study that asks the right question. The error taxonomy analysis is its strongest contribution. However, the gap between what the paper claims (two orthogonal degradation channels; confusability as an independent predictor with practical implications) and what the evidence supports (a tiny pairwise signal, one strong task-specific finding on RE, and several null or contradictory results elsewhere) is too large. The typical-batch confusability effect of ~2pp, combined with the failed NER cross-model validation, non-significant factorial test, and non-significant batch composition result, suggests that confusability -- while real -- is a minor factor compared to type count, prompt design, and other uncontrolled variables. For a top venue, the contribution needs either stronger effects or a method that successfully exploits the diagnostic signal. The paper is close but not there yet.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** (1) Drop or substantially temper the \"orthogonal\" framing -- present the two channels as an observational pattern, not a validated decomposition. (2) Lead with the error taxonomy as the main contribution rather than the pairwise correlations. (3) Provide a properly powered interaction test. (4) Replace the oracle-routed RE experiment with a realistic routing baseline. (5) Add a mid-scale model for NER Channel 2 validation.]]>"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4270,"cache_read_input_tokens":72194,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4270,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MeJqX3oCo7TesFn7RTGZr7","session_id":"cb6965cd-db2a-41dc-ac23-0e72d1208059","uuid":"00967712-65b1-4a90-b12c-277b2d3851fd"}
